The 108.000–137.000 MHz VHF aviation band is divided by FAA and FCC band plans into specific functional blocks rather than random assignments.
Here is the exact structural breakdown of the entire VHF airband—including where Company/ARINC, ACARS, VDL Mode 2, Guard, Flight Service, and ATC live.

1. Navigation Band: 108.000 – 117.975 MHz

• 108.000 – 111.950 MHz (Even tenths = VOR, Odd tenths = ILS Localizer): Frequencies with an even first decimal (e.g., 108.20, 110.40) are terminal VORs; odd first decimals (108.10, 109.50) are ILS Localizers.
• 112.000 – 117.950 MHz: En route VOR / VORTAC navigation aids and GBAS (Ground-Based Augmentation System) differential GPS data links.

2. Air Traffic Control & General Aviation: 118.000 – 128.800 MHz

While ATC assignments overlap somewhat across the band, the FAA partitions this block into distinct operational sub-bands:
• 118.000 – 121.400 MHz (Terminal ATC — Tower, Ground, Clearance, Approach): Primarily local Airport Traffic Control Towers (ATCT), Ground Control, Clearance Delivery, ATIS, and terminal TRACON sectors.
• 121.500 MHz (Emergency Guard): Protected international aeronautical emergency, distress, and lost-comms frequency (with buffer guard bands on 121.475 and 121.525).
• 121.600 – 121.925 MHz (Airport Surface / Ground Control): Dedicated low-power surface movement frequencies (Ground Control, Ramp Metering, Pre-Taxi Clearance) designed for short-range reuse at nearby airports.
• 121.950 – 123.075 MHz (Flight Service, Unicom, Multicom & General Aviation):
	• 122.000 – 122.050 MHz: En Route Flight Advisory Service (EFAS / Flight Watch — legacy weather).
	• 122.100 – 122.650 MHz: Flight Service Station (FSS) Remote Communications Outlets (RCOs) for flight plans and weather briefings.
	• 122.700 – 123.075 MHz: Non-towered airport CTAF/Unicom (122.700, 122.725, 122.800, 122.950, 123.000, 123.050), air-to-air (122.750 fixed-wing, 123.025 helicopter), and Multicom (122.900).
• 123.100 MHz (Search & Rescue / SAR): Protected national SAR scene-of-action and USCG/Civil Air Patrol coordination frequency.
• 123.125 – 123.575 MHz (Flight Test, Aviation Instruction & Gliders): Aircraft manufacturer flight testing (123.150–123.550), flight school operational channels, and sailplane/glider air-to-air (123.300, 123.500).
• 123.600 – 128.800 MHz (TRACON & ARTCC En Route ATC): Heavily populated by terminal approach/departure radar sectors and Air Route Traffic Control Center (ARTCC) low/high altitude sectors, alongside a few local towers and ATIS broadcasts.

3. Aeronautical Operational Control (AOC) — Company & ARINC: 128.825 – 132.000 MHz

This entire 3.175 MHz block is reserved exclusively for airline company operations, dispatch, FBOs, and ARINC/SITA data & voice relays—never FAA air traffic control. Inside this block, channels are split between voice and digital data:
• 128.825 – 128.975 MHz (ARINC Domestic En Route Voice): High-power regional network stations (like 128.900 MHz San Francisco Radio that you logged) providing long-range phone patches, MedLink medical calls, and dispatch relays.
• 129.000 – 132.000 MHz (Airline Company Voice & FBO Ops): Assigned to individual airlines (American, Delta, United, Southwest, Alaska, JetBlue, SkyWest, etc.) and major FBOs (Signature, Atlantic) for local station operations, ramp control, gate coordination, and line maintenance.
• Embedded ACARS & VDL Mode 2 Data Channels (Inside 129–136 MHz): Because your custom SDR skips ACARS, here are the exact digital data channels embedded inside and just above this band in North America so you can keep them permanently notched/ignored in your channelizer:
	• Legacy ACARS (2400 bps MSK AM):
		• 131.550 MHz — Primary North American ACARS channel
		• 130.025 MHz — Secondary North American ACARS channel
		• 129.125 MHz — Additional North American ACARS channel
		• 130.425 MHz / 130.450 MHz — Additional North American ACARS channels
		• 131.125 MHz / 131.725 MHz — Additional / overflow ACARS channels
	• Newer VDL Mode 2 (31.5 kbps D8PSK — CPDLC & High-Speed ACARS):
		• 136.975 MHz — Worldwide VDL Mode 2 Common Signaling Channel (CSC)
		• 136.650 MHz — North American ARINC VDL Mode 2 primary
		• 136.800 MHz — North American SITA VDL Mode 2 primary

4. Upper ATC & Digital Expansion Band: 132.025 – 136.975 MHz

• 132.025 – 135.975 MHz (ARTCC High/Low Sectors & Terminal ATC): Primarily dedicated to high-altitude and low-altitude en route Air Route Traffic Control Center sectors, along with busy terminal approach sectors, ATIS broadcasts (like KSAN 134.800), and occasional military discrete channels.
• 136.000 – 136.475 MHz (ATC / Special Use): Reserved for terminal ATC, ATIS, and specialized aviation services.
• 136.500 – 136.975 MHz (AOC Expansion & VDL Mode 2 Data): The top 500 kHz of the airband was opened later and is split between additional airline company voice channels (like JetBlue on 136.500 MHz) and the high-speed VDL Mode 2 digital data channels (136.650, 136.800, 136.975 MHz).

